<page>
<title>True / False</title>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            (8 points) Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and <m>\vec b\in \mathbb R ^{m}</m> unless otherwise stated. Select <em>true</em> if the statement is true for all choices of <m>A</m> and <m>\vec b</m>.  Otherwise, select <em>false</em>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="true">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If the system <m>A \vec x = \vec0</m> has infinitely many solutions, then the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly dependent. 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            True.
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Solution text
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> has a pivot in every row, then the equation <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> must have a unique solution.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>    
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>T ( \vec x ) = A \vec x </m> is a linear transformation that is both one-to-one and onto, then <m>A</m> must be a square matrix.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; The set of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> that satisfy <m>10x_1 - 3x_2 + x_3 = 0</m> is a line in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
            
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A, B, </m> and <m>C</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices such that <m>AC = BC</m> and <m>C</m> is not the zero matrix, then it must be the case that <m>A = B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec{v_1}</m> and <m>\vec{v_2}</m> are solutions to the inhomogeneous equation <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m>, then the vector <m>\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2}</m> is not a solution to <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> is a  <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix such that <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> is consistent for every <m>\vec b = \mathbb{R}^3</m>, then the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                True.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2} \}</m> and <m>b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3} \}</m>, then <m>b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_3} \}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                False.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</problem>


</page>


<page>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           (4 points) Indicate whether the following situations are possible or impossible. 
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; Four linearly independent vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Impossible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A linear transformation <m>T</m> such that <m>\vec{v_1}</m> and <m>\vec{v_2}</m> are linearly independent, but <m>T(\vec{v_1})</m> and <m>T(\vec{v_2})</m> are linearly dependent.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Possible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution Text.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A one-to-one linear transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A \vec{x}</m> such that the homogeneous system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has non-trivial solutions.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Impossible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m> 2 \times 3</m> matrix <m>A</m> and a vector <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^2</m> so that the solutions set of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a single point.

            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Impossible.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</problem>

<p workspace="0.5in">
    
</p>

<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (2 points) Let <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> be a consistent inhomogeneous linear system where 
                <me>
                        A=\begin{bmatrix}
                        2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3\\
                        -1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 1\\
                        5 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 1
                        \end{bmatrix}
                </me>
            <m>\textit{Select all that apply}</m>
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><m>A\vec{x}=\vec{0}</m> has the only trivial solution.</li>
            <li><m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^4</m> and <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>.</li>
            <li>The solution set of the system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is parallel to the solution set of the homogeneous system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m></li>
            <li>If <m>\vec{p}</m> is a solution of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, then <m>\vec{w} = \vec{p} + \vec{v_h}</m> is the general solution of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b} </m> where <m>\vec{v_h}</m> is any solution of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> </li>
        </ol>

    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            Options (b), (c), and (d) are correct.
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Option (a) is not correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (b) is correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (c) is correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (d) is correct.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>
    
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (2 points) Let <m>A</m> be a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix that has linearly dependent columns. Which of the following are true?

                <m>\textit{Select all that apply}</m>

                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><m>Ax = b</m> is consistent for every <m>b</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</li>
                        <li>the RREF of <m>A</m> has a zero row.</li>
                        <li>the columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\mathbb{R^3}</m>.</li>
                        <li>the system <m>A x = 0</m> has a free variable</li>
                    </ol>

            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Options (b) and (d) are correct.
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Option (a) is not correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (b) is correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (c) is not correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (d) is correct.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>    
                (3 points) Fill in the missing entries so that the vector <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to the system
                <me>
                    2x_1 + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } x_2 - x_3 = 2
                </me>
                <me>
                    \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } x_1 - 2x_2 = -1
                </me>
                <me> 
                    4x_1 + 2x_3 = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The correct equations are:
                <me>
                    2x_1 + 0 x_2 - x_3 = 2
                </me>
                <me>
                    3 x_1 - 2x_2 = -1
                </me>
                <me> 
                    4x_1 + 2x_3 = 4
                </me>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text.
            </p>
            </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (3 points) Consider the following system of linear equations. 
            </p>
            <me>
            x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3 = 7
            </me>
            <me>
                -2x_2 - 4x_3 = -6
            </me>
            <me>
            x_1 + 2x_2 + 5x_3 = 11
            </me>
            <p>
                Suppose the system is written in matrix form as <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. Express <m>\vec{b}</m> as a linear combination of the columns of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -6 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}
            </me>
            
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m> \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -6 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} = 3 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} - 1 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + 2 \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} </m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (4 points) Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp b \\ 2 \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>. Find <m>b</m> and <m>d</m> so that <m>AB = BA</m>
                    <me>
                        b = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }

                        d = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                    </me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>b = 1</m> and <m>d = 2</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (3 points) Write a nonzero matrix <m>A</m> of size <m>3 \times 3</m> which is in echelon form (REF) and such that the vector <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> and such that the vector <m>\vec{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> causes <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> to be consistent. 
            </p>

            <p>
                <me> A = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ a \\ a \\ a  \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <me> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (2 points) Fill in the blank so that the systems with the two augmented matrices below have the same solution set.

                <me> 
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp \rvert \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp -3 \amp \rvert \amp -3 \end{bmatrix}
                </me> and
                <me> 
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp \rvert \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp \rvert \amp  \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }\end{bmatrix}
                </me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <me> \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp \rvert \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp \rvert \amp  1 \end{bmatrix}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                solution text.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

</page>

<page>
    <problem>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                (7 points) <em>Show your work for part (c) in the space below and put your answer in the boxes.</em> 
            </p>
            <p>
                Let <m>T</m> be the linear transformation defined by 
                <me>T \begin{pmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 - x_3 \\ 2x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \end{bmatrix}</me>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What are the domain and codomain of <m>T</m>?
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>\text{Domain is }\framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ a \\ a \\ a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>\text{Codomain is }\framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ a \\ a \\ a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Domain is <m>\mathbb{R^3}</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Codomain is <m>\mathbb{R^2}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution Text
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="true">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix <m>A</m> such that <m>T(\vec{x}) = A \vec{x}</m> for all <m>\vec{x}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>A = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
            </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text 
                </p> 
            </solution>
            

        </task>

        <task workspace="true">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a vector <m>\vec{x}</m> such that <m>T ( \vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> where <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix}3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>\vec{x} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
            </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text 
                </p> 
            </solution>
            

        </task>

        <task workspace="true">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>T</m> one-to-one?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    no
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text 
                </p> 
            </solution>
            

        </task>

        <task workspace="true">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>T</m> onto?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    yes
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text 
                </p> 
            </solution>
            

        </task>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                (6 points) <em>Show your work in the space below and put your answer in the boxes.</em> 
            </p>
            <p>
                Find all <m>h</m> such that the following set of vectors is linearly dependent. For that <m>h</m>, find a non-trivial linear combination of the vectors that adds to zero. 
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},\begin{pmatrix}h \\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} \right\}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>h = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>\framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a\amp a \\ a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>h = 3</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>-2 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end {bmatrix} + 3 \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1\\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + 1 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text:
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                (6 points) <em>Show your work for part (a) in the space below and put your answer in the boxes.</em> 
            </p>
            <p>
                Consider the linear system of equations <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> where
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</me> and <me>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix}5 \\ -5 \end{bmatrix}</me>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give the general solution of the system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> in parametric vector form.
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>\vec{x} =  \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ a \\ a \\ a  \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + r \begin{bmatrix}-3 \\ -1 \\ 1 \\0 \end{bmatrix} + t \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}; r,t \in \mathbb{R}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>A</m> be as defined above. Write down the solution set to the homogeneous system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> as the span of some number of vectors. <m>\textit{(Hint: You may use your solution from (a) above.)}</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                <me>\framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \amp a \amp a \amp a \\ a \\ a \\ a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } </me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>Span \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ -1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} , \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Solution text.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </problem>
</page>

</handout>
